WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.854
- Almost all the letters that get sent
in the United States

00:00:02.854 --> 00:00:04.110
are processed automatically.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.060
You put a letter in a post box,

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.350
it's taken to the closest
processing center,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.318
where cameras and computers take a picture
of the front of the envelope,

00:00:10.318 --> 00:00:13.950
read the address with optical
character recognition, OCR,

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.890
and it's then passed on to
the right truck or plane.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:20.000
That deals with pretty much everything,
even the handwritten letters.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.820
But what if your
handwriting is really bad?

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.170
Like, really bad.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.930
Or the envelope got a bit damp,
and the ink blurred and ran?

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.720
The postal service sends the
picture of the envelope here

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.350
to the Remote Encoding Center, the REC,
in Salt Lake City, Utah,

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.155
and it's the job of the folks here
to turn scrawl and blurred ink 

00:00:37.155 --> 00:00:38.460
into actual addresses.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:39.720
It's a quiet day here today.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.460
Mail volumes are low in
the middle of summer.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.846
So while it's not busy inside,

00:00:42.846 --> 00:00:45.840
I'm going to see if I'd
be any good at this.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.560
- We're in the last Remote Encoding Center
in the United States.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.311
Back in 1997, when we had 55 RECs open,

00:00:52.311 --> 00:00:55.968
all of those RECs combined
keyed 19 billion images.

00:00:55.968 --> 00:00:59.484
The OCR technology is
so good that, in 2021,

00:00:59.484 --> 00:01:03.810
as the last REC remaining,
we only keyed 1.2 billion images.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:07.064
Right now, we have about
810 employees here.

00:01:07.064 --> 00:01:09.150
Shortly, we're going
to be teaching Tom how

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.560
to process some of this mail.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.011
It'll be a lot of fun.

00:01:12.011 --> 00:01:15.420
- It's important to say that I can't
show you any actual mail,

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:16.785
not the envelopes, not anything.

00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:19.020
If any appears in the
background on a screen,

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.835
it'll be blurred out.
That's all considered strictly private.

00:01:21.835 --> 00:01:25.050
And besides, I'm not going to
flash random people's addresses

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.340
on the screen of a YouTube video,

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.540
particularly when they'll
usually be written

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:28.413
in distinctive handwriting?

00:01:28.413 --> 00:01:30.780
You can probably work out
how that could go wrong.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.730
But there is a stack
of demonstration mail,

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.380
envelopes and addresses used for training,

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.215
and that's what I'm going to be tested on.

00:01:36.215 --> 00:01:37.890
- If you drop off a piece of mail,

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.698
it makes it to the local processing plant.

00:01:39.698 --> 00:01:41.952
If the machine can't
read your handwriting,

00:01:41.952 --> 00:01:45.002
one of the keyers here will
type in the missing information,

00:01:45.002 --> 00:01:47.400
and the information goes
right back to the plant,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.490
stays within automation,
which is a whole lot cheaper

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.780
than having somebody physically
sort that piece by hand.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.826
- All right, what do I need to do?

00:01:56.826 --> 00:01:59.670
- Put your last name and
first initial into that.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.050
- I looked at the keyboard
to look for the numbers

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.100
and realized this is a-

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.710
- That is not a standard keyboard!
- That is not a standard keyboard.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.390
Other than the QWERTY
letters and the numpad,

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:07.442
everything is different. Okay.

00:02:07.442 --> 00:02:09.906
- To make everything faster for us,

00:02:09.906 --> 00:02:12.690
they did some weird
things with the keyboards

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:13.572
that they've provided us.

00:02:13.572 --> 00:02:18.870
The home row, A, S, D, F, those
also work as your numbers.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.310
You can't reach up to the
top of the keyboard to hit 1, 2, 3.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:25.800
- "How to key the outward
portion of a mailpiece image."

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.840
- We're calling outward
the city and the state.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.070
- City and the state, okay.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.065
Siemens came up with this in the '90s.

00:02:31.065 --> 00:02:34.925
So they decided "C coding"
is going to mean zip code.

00:02:34.925 --> 00:02:37.287
Outward is going to mean city and state.

00:02:37.287 --> 00:02:40.328
Inward is going to mean
the street address.

00:02:40.328 --> 00:02:42.660
- And that's now the
language that you use,

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:43.642
because that's what they decided.

00:02:43.642 --> 00:02:46.420
- To make it faster to key these letters,

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.130
our keying is a small extract
of what's on the letter,

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:53.543
and then it compares it to the
known-good addresses in the database.

00:02:53.543 --> 00:02:55.741
Every known-good address
in America is sitting

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:57.780
on our servers in the back.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.159
If it's good, the piece just
goes away, and you're done.

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:02.280
If it matches a couple of good addresses,

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.227
then you'll get a list and
you'll choose out of that list,

00:03:04.227 --> 00:03:06.045
which is the address you were trying for.

00:03:06.045 --> 00:03:09.240
It makes it a whole lot faster
than typing out the entire things.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.630
- "Key in the first three characters
of the first word in the city name."

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.550
Okay, first character of the second word.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.779
Two character state or
territory of abbreviation.

00:03:16.779 --> 00:03:20.430
- It's the 3+1 rule. It's the same
for the street addresses.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.096
- Okay. "Press the Go key when ready."

00:03:22.096 --> 00:03:22.929
Go.

00:03:24.435 --> 00:03:27.767
So that is LE TMS.

00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:29.603
- Oh. 3+1 rule.

00:03:29.603 --> 00:03:31.825
If there's not three characters,
you got to do a space.

00:03:31.825 --> 00:03:32.820
- Ah, okay.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:34.410
- Now, you've got to clear
it out with the Enter key.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.364
- So that is LE, space, TMS.

00:03:37.364 --> 00:03:41.393
- C/S key.
- C/S, oh, "city/state". Got it, okay.

00:03:41.393 --> 00:03:43.348
- Some pieces are buffered
and some pieces are live.

00:03:43.348 --> 00:03:46.448
That live mail will be
sitting on a conveyor belt,

00:03:46.448 --> 00:03:48.510
going around the machine.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:52.260
Those flats and those packages,
if we don't key them in 90 seconds

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.000
and get the information
back to that machine,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.530
it dumps it off into a reject bin

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.390
and somebody has to hand sort that.

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:00.060
So for the letters, they run them
through the machine one time,

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.470
they store them off to the side.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:02.640
A few hours later,

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.152
they put them in the
machine the second time,

00:04:04.152 --> 00:04:07.392
anticipating that we've processed
everything that was missing.

00:04:07.392 --> 00:04:11.454
- Oh. I'm guessing that's, like...
"Dixon Hill"? Or something like that.

00:04:11.454 --> 00:04:13.269
I can't tell if that's DIC or DX...

00:04:13.269 --> 00:04:15.053
- Unless it was...
- Ox Hill! It's Ox Hill.

00:04:17.674 --> 00:04:18.900
Okay.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:19.733
Oh.

00:04:19.733 --> 00:04:21.937
- Remember, these are the
easiest ones to read on the test.

00:04:21.937 --> 00:04:22.890
- I realized that.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:23.760
- Get ready.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.341
Every year, we have
fewer and fewer pieces.

00:04:26.341 --> 00:04:29.187
We've got fewer people handwriting mail,

00:04:29.187 --> 00:04:32.228
and we have OCR technology
that's really good.

00:04:32.228 --> 00:04:34.650
I'll bet we went from
somewhere in the neighborhood

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:38.339
of less than half of the mail being
read by a computer in the '90s

00:04:38.339 --> 00:04:42.152
to nearly 99% of letters
being read today.

00:04:42.152 --> 00:04:45.899
The OCRs that are reading the letter mail,
I think it's close to 99%.

00:04:45.899 --> 00:04:48.148
So we get the 1% of the junk,

00:04:48.148 --> 00:04:52.740
and we improve about
half of that 1%. - Right.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.730
- The job here may be getting harder
because pieces are harder to read.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.310
Because if the computer is
so good and can't read it,

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:00.600
it's a lot more likely,

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.280
it's not a good piece
of mail to begin with.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.380
The address was destroyed somehow,

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:08.160
or the customer had bad
information for the address.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.914
- So they've put "Ft" Lauderdale
in the abbreviation.

00:05:10.914 --> 00:05:13.292
So I would put F-T, no matter what?
Not F-O-R?

00:05:13.292 --> 00:05:17.070
I don't autocorrect that.
- Correct. Do it how the customer did it.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.957
We're not sleuths.
We're not picking out details.

00:05:19.957 --> 00:05:21.599
You key by the rules,

00:05:21.599 --> 00:05:25.878
you type in the address information
from what you can clearly see.

00:05:25.878 --> 00:05:28.410
Now, the street address works similarly,

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.754
where you'll do all the
numbers, and then 3+1.

00:05:30.754 --> 00:05:33.755
- "Ignore the direction word
and key the street name.

00:05:33.755 --> 00:05:37.440
"If the direction word is
the only word, key it."

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:38.550
Okay. 3+1 rule.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.086
So, Go. All right.

00:05:41.086 --> 00:05:44.588
Ah, the... I looked down to find
where the numbers are!

00:05:44.588 --> 00:05:48.966
720WES, no, yeah, that's West Boulevard.

00:05:48.966 --> 00:05:51.720
So you include that and you hit Street.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:54.900
400 Little Way Street.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.207
- We have internet service
from three different providers.

00:05:57.207 --> 00:06:01.740
We have three fiber optic lines coming
into the building at different points.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.989
So if any one point gets chopped off,
we can maintain service,

00:06:04.989 --> 00:06:08.253
because we're the only REC,

00:06:08.253 --> 00:06:12.110
connected to every plant in the country,
over 300 processing plants,

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:16.500
including Guam and
Anchorage, Alaska; and Juneau, Alaska;

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:17.924
and San Juan, Puerto Rico.

00:06:17.924 --> 00:06:20.310
It's asking for numeric,
and it's in C coding,

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:21.630
so it wants the zip code.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.443
- It wants the zip code, 35804, go.

00:06:23.443 --> 00:06:25.620
- So if both the machine can't read it

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.105
and a keyer isn't able
to decipher it here,

00:06:28.105 --> 00:06:30.974
the machine at the plant
sends it to the reject bin,

00:06:30.974 --> 00:06:33.060
and then somebody will manually handle it.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.531
And then, if they can't figure it out,

00:06:34.531 --> 00:06:36.450
it would either get
returned to the sender.

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.050
And if there's no
return-to-sender information,

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:40.537
then it will go to the
Mail Recovery Center.

00:06:40.537 --> 00:06:41.739
- Okay, final test.

00:06:41.739 --> 00:06:42.572
Here we go.

00:06:42.572 --> 00:06:43.416
Numeric.

00:06:43.416 --> 00:06:44.780
We don't have a numeric.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:46.408
So we hit None.

00:06:46.408 --> 00:06:47.241
- Correct.

00:06:47.241 --> 00:06:51.558
- Then, it's outward, which is MOK, space,

00:06:51.558 --> 00:06:59.330
HI, C/S, 2545HEAD... Street.

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:01.290
- Nice.
- Okay.

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:03.210
I mean, what speed
would I have to go here?

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:05.348
- 7,150 keystrokes per hour.

00:07:05.348 --> 00:07:09.390
- It's the speed with which
you have to read that, parse it...

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.730
It's much tougher than I
thought it was going to be

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:13.500
to keep everything in your head.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.624
And this is easy handwriting.

00:07:14.624 --> 00:07:16.110
Nothing here has been blurred.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:17.370
- And you haven't even gotten to the ones

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:19.035
that are upside down or backwards, right,

00:07:19.035 --> 00:07:21.734
or packages that have four sides to them.

00:07:21.734 --> 00:07:24.654
- And it would be an
average of four seconds

00:07:24.654 --> 00:07:26.488
for each one to keep up the pace.

00:07:26.488 --> 00:07:28.710
- On average, it's about four seconds

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.600
that somebody's got one
of these on their screen.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:31.433
- Wow.

00:07:31.433 --> 00:07:33.360
- And you did an incredible job

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.160
for just learning these rules right now.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:35.993
I'm impressed.

00:07:35.993 --> 00:07:38.312
- All right, thank you.
I'll take that!

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:41.793
Thank you so much.

